1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,625
Global Marathon Boom Continues With Record Participation Numbers.

2
00:00:04,575 --> 00:00:18,550
The global running phenomenon showed no signs of slowing in 2026, with the six Abbott World Marathon Majors registering a combined 525,000 finishers, a 14% increase over 2025.

3
00:00:18,550 --> 00:00:29,750
The Boston Marathon reached its largest field in history with 40,000 runners, while the New York City Marathon capped entries at 55,000 within eight hours of registration opening.

4
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:38,575
Women's marathon participation hit a record 43% globally, driven by grassroots programs like World Athletics' "She Runs" initiative.

5
00:00:38,575 --> 00:00:53,562
Kenya's Kelvin Kiptum lowered his own world record to 1:59:16 at the Berlin Marathon, becoming the first athlete to break the 2-hour barrier in a legitimate, record-eligible race — a milestone once thought impossible.

6
00:00:53,562 --> 00:01:02,425
On the women's side, Ethiopia's Tigst Assefa clocked 2:11:53 in Chicago, shattering her own mark.

7
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:18,225
The boom extended beyond elite racing: charity runners raised an estimated $2.4 billion across the six majors, and virtual marathon platforms allowed runners in 147 countries to participate remotely through connected fitness devices.

8
00:01:18,225 --> 00:01:29,675
The Paris 2024 Olympic marathon's legacy, combined with increased investment in urban running infrastructure, has fueled a sustained surge in endurance sports participation worldwide.

